\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The logarithm on the accretive cone}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{E} be a finite-dimensional complex Hermitian space, and
let #{\mathcal C} be the open convex cone of strictly accretive
endomorphisms of #{E}, in the sense of \ref{fdip-009K}. There
is a unique holomorphic function #{\mathcal L} on #{\mathcal C}
such that
##{\ue^{\mathcal L(K)}=\det K,\qquad \mathcal L(\bbI)=0.}
For #{K_t=\bbI+t(K-\bbI)}, it is given by
##{\mathcal L(K)=\int_0^1
 \trace\left[K_t^{-1}(K-\bbI)\right]dt.}
On Hermitian positive operators it equals the real
#{\log\det K}. Thus the fermionic logarithmic contribution
#{W_F(K)=-\mathcal L(K)} is unambiguous on this cone. This
branch need not be the principal scalar logarithm of
#{\det K}. \ref{fdip-00MI} records the values
#{4\pi\ui /3} and #{-2\pi\ui /3}, the winding obstruction, and the
singular determinant.}

\proof{
\p{Write #{K_t=\bbI+t(K-\bbI)}. Convexity makes #{K_t} strictly
accretive, hence invertible, for every #{0\le t\le1}. In a
neighborhood of each #{K\in\mathcal C}, the Hermitian parts of
all these operators have a common positive lower bound. Their
inverses remain locally bounded uniformly in #{t}. Inversion,
the trace, and this integral are holomorphic operator
operations on that neighborhood, so #{\mathcal L} is
holomorphic.}

\p{Put #{g(t)=\det K_t}. Choose an orthonormal basis and
represent the operators by their matrices in that basis. The
determinant derivative, as in the
[real log-determinant calculation](fdip-005S), gives
##{\frac{g'(t)}{g(t)}
 =\trace(K_t^{-1}(K-\bbI)).}
This complex version follows from
#{\det(K_t+hB)=\det K_t\det(\bbI+hK_t^{-1}B)} and from the
permutation expansion
#{\det(\bbI+hM)=1+h\trace M+O(h^2)}. Therefore the
derivative of
#{g(t)\ue^{-\int_0^t g'(s)/g(s)\,ds}} is zero. Its value at
zero is one, proving #{\ue^{\mathcal L(K)}=\det K}. The identity
operator gives #{\mathcal L(\bbI)=0}.}

\p{Two such logarithms differ by a continuous function with
values in #{2\pi\ui\mathbb Z}. The cone is connected, so this
function is constant, and normalization makes it zero. On a
Hermitian positive operator, every #{K_t} is Hermitian
positive and #{g(t)>0}; ordinary real logarithmic
differentiation identifies the integral with #{\log\det K}.}
}

\transclude{fdip-00MI}
